<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">UK house prices have fallen on an annual b=
asis for the first time in almost three years, as the housing market is hit=
 by rising borrowing costs and geopolitical uncertainty.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Lender Lloyds has reported this morning th=
at house prices fell by 0.4% in August, compared with a year ago, which is =
the first year-on-year decrease since November 2023.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">On a monthly basis, prices fell by 0.2% in=
 August, after a 0.1% drop in July, meaning the average property now costs =
=C2=A3298,468.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This is weaker than expected =E2=80=93 eco=
nomists had forecast a 0.1% monthly rise, and a 0.2% increase compared with=
 a year ago.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Andrew Asaam, mortgages director at Lloyds=
, explains that the market remains subdued in August: =E2=80=9CThe housing =
market has faced a more difficult backdrop in recent months, with the impac=
t of global events on inflation and borrowing costs creating greater econom=
ic uncertainty.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CWhat we=E2=80=99re not seeing is =
a rush of homeowners cutting prices. But more are choosing to sit tight, wi=
th sellers reluctant to accept offers they feel are too low, while some buy=
ers are waiting to see how conditions develop.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CAs a result, fewer homes are chan=
ging hands, with latest industry figures showing mortgage approvals now at =
their lowest level since the start of 2024.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">And there may be worse to come =E2=80=93 a=
s the recent bond market turmoil has pushed up lenders=E2=80=99 borrowing c=
osts.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">That increase in =E2=80=9Cswap rates=E2=80=
=9D could make mortgages more expensive, leaving buyers with less firepower=
 in the market.<strong><br><br></strong></p>
